# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# A quoted name that begins with a slash is an ADDRESS in the
# namespace, not prose, and the colour says so: the path wears an ink
# of its own -- the string's, met halfway with the shade a chosen thing
# wears -- while a string that is NOT a path stays plain string red.
# Two glyph pixels, two rows apart, two inks: what is proven is that
# the classifier tells a path from a string, not merely that something
# was coloured.  The pixels were probed, and they are GLYPH pixels.
wait 25
click 980 30
wait 4
menu-pick Development
wait 4
menu-pick TikuBasic
wait 150
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Open Project…
wait 40
click 400 220
wait 6
click 675 413
wait 60
click-window Project
wait 8
key Down
wait 8
key Return
wait 90
expect-title main.bas
note the path's own ink, on a glyph of "/dev/led"
expect-pixel 268 323 69435c
note the plain string's red, on a glyph of "hello there" a row below
expect-pixel 288 337 a02020
note and the statement before them both is still the reserved word's blue
expect-pixel 241 320 0000b0
quit
